('print-on-demand-after-the-hype-whats-still-working', 'Print on Demand After the Hype: What''s Still Working', $e8$The get-rich-quick videos moved on to the next trend years ago. Here is an honest look at what print on demand actually pays in 2026.$e8$, $c8$
<p>Print on demand had its viral moment a few years back, promising a business you could run from your phone with zero inventory and zero upfront cost. The upfront cost part was always true. The effortless part never was, and the sellers still making real money from it now look very different from the ones the hype videos described.</p>
<h2>The design flooding problem is real</h2>
<p>Marketplaces built around print on demand are saturated with generic slogan designs, mass produced with almost no differentiation. A plain text design that would have sold reasonably in 2021 now competes against tens of thousands of near identical listings. The sellers still doing well have mostly moved away from generic slogans entirely and toward designs tied to a specific, identifiable niche audience, like a particular dog breed community or a specific profession's inside jokes.</p>
<h3>Margins are thinner than the videos suggested</h3>
<p>After base product cost, platform fees, and marketplace commission, a typical print on demand item nets somewhere between $2 and $6 profit per sale for most sellers, not the wide margins often implied. That math only works at meaningful volume, which means the actual business is more about consistent design output and smart listing optimization than about any single design going viral.</p>
<h2>What separates sellers who are still profitable</h2>
<ul>
<li>They build design collections around one specific audience rather than scattering random slogans across every possible niche.</li>
<li>They treat their own storefront and email list as a real channel, not relying entirely on marketplace search traffic.</li>
<li>They watch trend cycles closely and retire designs that stop performing instead of leaving hundreds of stale listings live.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Where the real opportunity moved</h2>
<p>Higher margin niches within print on demand, home decor, drinkware for specific hobby communities, and apparel for niche professional groups, still show healthy demand because they face less flooding than the T-shirt slogan category. Sellers who research a narrow community's specific language and inside references before designing tend to outperform sellers designing for a broad, generic audience by a wide margin.</p>
<h2>The time investment people underestimate</h2>
<p>Building a print on demand catalog that earns a meaningful side income, say $500 to $1,500 a month, typically takes 6 to 12 months of steady weekly design work before it compounds into that range. The early months look discouraging: a handful of sales on hundreds of listings. Sellers who quit at month two, before the catalog reaches critical mass, never see the compounding that makes month eight look completely different from month one.</p>
<h2>Is it still worth starting in 2026</h2>
<p>Print on demand is not the low effort side hustle it was marketed as, but it remains a genuinely low upfront risk way to test design ideas against a real market without holding inventory. The honest pitch now is this: expect thin per unit margins, expect to need real design differentiation rather than generic slogans, and expect the first few months to look unrewarding before volume and catalog size start to matter. Anyone selling you a faster or easier version of this is selling a course, not a realistic plan.</p>
<h2>Seasonal planning matters more than most sellers realize</h2>
<p>Designs tied to a specific holiday or seasonal moment need a lead time most new sellers underestimate, since marketplace search behavior for seasonal items starts weeks before the actual date and a design listed the week of an event has already missed most of its window. Building a seasonal calendar months in advance, and listing designs early enough to accumulate reviews and search visibility before peak demand hits, consistently outperforms reactive, last minute seasonal listings created after noticing a trend already underway.</p>
<h2>Customer service still matters even in a low touch business model</h2>
<p>Print on demand is often pitched as entirely hands off once a design is listed, but sizing questions, print quality concerns, and shipping delays from the fulfillment partner still generate real customer messages that need a real, timely response. Sellers who treat customer service as beneath their attention in this supposedly passive business model tend to accumulate exactly the kind of negative reviews that suppress future sales across their entire catalog, since marketplace algorithms in this category weigh seller responsiveness and review quality heavily when deciding which listings to surface to new searchers.</p>
$c8$, null, null, 'published', '2026-06-11T07:40:00Z'),
('notion-templates-that-actually-sell-structure-over-aesthetics', 'Notion Templates That Actually Sell: Structure Over Aesthetics', $e9$Pretty covers get the click. Clear structure gets the five star review. Here is what separates a Notion template that sells once from one that sells for years.$e9$, $c9$
<p>Notion template marketplaces are full of beautiful cover images attached to templates that fall apart the moment a real person tries to use them daily. The templates that keep selling for years, and keep getting good reviews, tend to look plainer on the surface and work much harder underneath.</p>
<h2>Start from a real workflow, not a database showcase</h2>
<p>Many templates are built to demonstrate every Notion feature at once: relations, rollups, formulas, linked databases, all stacked into one dashboard. That impresses in a screenshot and confuses in daily use. Templates that hold up over time are usually built around one specific, real workflow, tracking client projects for a freelancer, planning content for a small team, running a personal budget, and every feature included earns its place by serving that one workflow.</p>
<h3>Onboarding inside the template matters more than the demo video</h3>
<p>A buyer who opens their new template and sees an empty database with no guidance closes the tab within a minute. The templates with the best reviews include a short getting started page inside the template itself, explaining what to fill in first, with two or three example entries already populated so the structure makes sense at a glance rather than requiring a video to understand.</p>
<h2>Pricing follows complexity, not effort</h2>
<p>A simple, well built weekly planner template can reasonably sell for $9 to $15. A more complex system, a full client relationship manager with linked invoicing and project tracking, can justify $29 to $59, particularly if it saves the buyer from paying for a separate paid tool. The mistake many template makers make is pricing based on how long the template took to build rather than how much value or cost avoidance it delivers to the buyer.</p>
<h2>What actually generates repeat customers</h2>
<ul>
<li>A changelog page inside the template showing recent updates, which signals the product is maintained rather than abandoned.</li>
<li>A duplicate-friendly structure that does not break if the buyer reorganizes their own Notion workspace around it.</li>
<li>Clear instructions for customizing colors, properties, and views without needing any Notion expertise beyond basic clicking.</li>
</ul>
<h2>The mistake of over-customizing your own taste into the template</h2>
<p>Template makers often bake their own highly specific preferences, an unusual color scheme, a particular icon style, a specific naming convention, so deeply into the structure that buyers with different habits struggle to adapt it. The best selling templates leave more room for a buyer's existing habits, offering sensible defaults without forcing a rigid system the buyer has to fully abandon their previous habits to use.</p>
<h2>Where sales actually come from after launch</h2>
<p>Screenshots posted to relevant communities, not the marketplace search page alone, drive a surprising share of ongoing sales for successful template makers. A short post showing the template solving a specific, relatable problem, "how I finally stopped losing track of freelance invoices," tends to outperform generic promotional posts by a wide margin, because it demonstrates the actual value rather than describing it abstractly. Template sellers who treat this kind of organic demonstration as part of their regular routine, not a one time launch tactic, tend to see much steadier sales over the following year than those who rely on marketplace visibility alone.</p>
<h2>Cross platform compatibility gets overlooked until a refund arrives</h2>
<p>A template built and tested exclusively on desktop can behave unpredictably on Notion's mobile app, with certain layout choices, wide tables or deeply nested toggles in particular, becoming genuinely difficult to use on a smaller screen. Testing every template on both desktop and mobile before launch catches these problems early, and mentioning mobile compatibility explicitly in the listing, when it has actually been verified, addresses a real and common pre-purchase concern that otherwise goes unanswered until a frustrated buyer raises it after the fact.</p>
<h2>Bundling related templates into a system, later rather than first</h2>
<p>Once two or three individual templates targeting the same audience have proven themselves individually, packaging them together as a connected system, with shared navigation and a slightly higher combined price, tends to outperform continuing to sell them purely as separate products. This works best as a second stage move rather than a starting strategy, since building an ambitious connected system before any individual piece has proven real demand risks investing heavily in structure nobody specifically asked for yet.</p>
